From ff9efc2eab4804b0dd430744f7c70cbc20e1d661 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: f <f@f>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 23:03:53 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] media: iris: leave the core in the error state when its init
 is refused

iris_core_init() returns -EINVAL when the core is in IRIS_CORE_ERROR,
and went to the label that writes IRIS_CORE_DEINIT. Nothing was powered
off on that path: a core in the error state is still on, with its IRQ
enabled and its firmware loaded. The worker that recovers the core
begins with iris_core_deinit(), which powers off unless the state is
IRIS_CORE_DEINIT. So one open() in the window between the firmware's
error and that worker made it skip the power-off, and the
iris_core_init() that followed powered on a core that was on.

Read on the phone on 2026-11-26 (diting), with the kernel log captured
live: seventeen VA-API decoders started half a second apart, the
firmware's system error (0x5000003), the recovery, a second system
error 105 ms after the first, and 8 ms later the "core init failed" of
an open(). The next run of the worker then reported "Unbalanced enable
for IRQ 219" from iris_vpu_power_on(), 9.6 ms after that line: the
power-off's disable_irq() had not run. The firmware watchdog fired
2.2 s later, and opening the node twenty times more reset the SoC. The
"Unbalanced enable for IRQ 218" from iris_open() read the same morning,
after a system error, fits the same path.

The state is now left as it is: the open() gets its -EINVAL, and the
worker powers the core off before it brings it up again. Where nothing
had scheduled the worker - the firmware answering the system init with
an error, or not answering within a second - the refused open()
schedules it, so that the core is recovered instead of staying on in
the error state until a reboot.
---
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.c
index 56832df348..86f96b727a 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.c
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
+#include <linux/workqueue.h>
 
 #include "iris_core.h"
 #include "iris_firmware.h"
@@ -58,8 +59,15 @@ int iris_core_init(struct iris_core *core)
 		ret = 0;
 		goto exit;
 	} else if (core->state == IRIS_CORE_ERROR) {
+		/*
+		 * The core is still powered on. Only the error handler
+		 * clears this state, and it powers the core off first.
+		 */
+		if (!delayed_work_pending(&core->sys_error_handler))
+			schedule_delayed_work(&core->sys_error_handler,
+					      msecs_to_jiffies(10));
 		ret = -EINVAL;
-		goto error;
+		goto exit;
 	}
 
 	core->state = IRIS_CORE_INIT;
